    "text": "Let RS be the diameter of the circle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math> is the point<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>be a variable point (other than&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>S</mi></math>) on the circle and tangents to the circle at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>meet at the point<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>. The normal to the circle at P intersects a line drawn through<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math> parallel to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> at point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> . Then, the locus of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> passes through the point (s):",
